<article class="env-lemma" id="018U">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/018U">Lemma <span data-tag="018U">14.21.10</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. Let $U$ be a simplicial object of $\mathcal{A}$. Let $n \geq 0$ be an integer. The morphism $i_{n!} \text{sk}_ n U \to U$ identifies $i_{n!} \text{sk}_ n U$ with the simplicial subobject $U' \subset U$ defined in Lemma <a href="/tag/017Y" data-tag="017Y">14.18.9</a>. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/018T" data-tag="018T">14.21.9</a> we have $N(i_{n!} \text{sk}_ n U)_ i = 0$ for $i > n$. The map $i_{n!} \text{sk}_ n U \to U$ is an isomorphism in degrees $\leq n$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/018N" data-tag="018N">14.21.3</a>. Combined we conclude that the map $i_{n!} \text{sk}_ n U \to U$ induces injective maps $N(i_{n!} \text{sk}_ n U)_ i \to N(U)_ i$ for all $i$. Hence Lemma <a href="/tag/017W" data-tag="017W">14.18.7</a> applies. Thus $i_{n!} \text{sk}_ n U \to U$ is injective. The result follows easily from this. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>